Project SISYPHUS-Prime v1 Initial Design Summary

Project Goals:

Build a dual-action recombinant fusion protein that degrades bacterial biofilm extracellular polymeric substance (EPS) matrices and lyses the underlying pathogen cells.

Connect an alginate lyase enzymatic core to an antimicrobial peptide (AMP) payload while avoiding steric hindrance using a flexible linker.

Include an N-terminal PelB leader sequence for periplasmic secretion and a C-terminal 6xHis tag for nickel affinity purification.

Validate the construct entirely in silico via ESMFold and PyMOL to confirm structural stability (pLDDT > 80) before wet-lab execution.

Initial Design Process:

Primary Sequence Assembly: Combining the PelB leader, splice alginate lyase, (GGGGS)3 linker, FK-13 AMP, and 6xHis tag into a linear N-to-C terminal FASTA sequence.

Original and first deisgn using a PelB leader and a 6xHis tag to be able to extract protein from an e-coli host (pET-28a) real protein is just alignate lyase through fk-13

Once protein passes all tests it will be translated into a plasmid.

Genetic Circuit & Codon Optimization: Translating the protein sequence into a pET-28a expression vector using Benchling, optimizing codons for E. coli expression, and checking restriction sites.

AI Structure Prediction: Running the FASTA sequence through ESMFold in Google Colab to generate 3D coordinate pdb files.

PyMOL Structural Validation: Inspecting the model in PyMOL to verify pLDDT scores, check surface electrostatic charges, and confirm that the catalytic active site remains unblocked by the AMP domain.

